#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
ARM ANALYZER NODE v0  -  "what is the arm doing right now, and is it healthy?"   (PRISM, Raspberry Pi 4)
                                                  Sister of belt_analyzer_node.py (the CONVEYOR analyzer).

Measure -> Observe -> Record -> Report. It NEVER commands an actuator. Only the Orchestrator may act on what it says.

INPUTS  (subscribed straight from the broker, not through the Logger)
  arm/telemetry    10 Hz  joint pos / speed / current / temp, as autoen_node.py or arm_node.py publish it
                   (pos in ticks; cur in current-register counts: MA_PER_COUNT below turns them into mA; temp in degC,
                    may be missing or slow: the last value is kept for TEMP_STALE_S)
  arm/gripper      G events from the Mega: CLOSING / CLOSED / ERROR       arm/ir  retained IR level at the pick point
  arm/status, camera/demand, camera/perception, health/status, belt/telemetry   kept in the store for context

OUTPUTS
  analyzer/arm/state        retained   state + context + condition levels per joint (now on change, else 1 Hz)
  analyzer/arm/transition   QoS1       one message per state change
  analyzer/arm/cycle        QoS1       one message per finished pick-and-place cycle
  analyzer/arm/condition    QoS1       a current / temp level changed on one joint (OK <-> WARN <-> ALARM)
  analyzer/arm/status       retained   online flag + Last Will (so a dead analyzer is visible)
  arm_analyzer.db           SQLite     tables: transitions, cycles, conditions

STATES  (derived ONLY from where the arm is, whether it moves, and the gripper opening)
  IDLE         at HOME, still                             waiting for the next cycle
  APPROACHING  moving HOME -> STANDBY, gripper open
  WAITING      at STANDBY, still, gripper open            waiting for the block (belt running)
  PICKING      at STANDBY, gripper closing / closed       grabbing
  CARRYING     gripper closed, arm between poses          detail says which bin
  PLACING      at a bin (left_bin / right_bin)            releasing
  RETURNING    gripper open, moving bin -> HOME
  MOVING       arm moves but the context is unknown (e.g. analyzer started mid-motion)
  FAULT        the Mega reported G,ERROR (grip NOT confirmed)
  NO_DATA      no fresh arm/telemetry. "I cannot tell", not "the machine is broken".

A normal cycle is IDLE > APPROACHING > WAITING > PICKING > CARRYING > PLACING > RETURNING > IDLE.
Any other jump is flagged "unexpected", and any joint 1-5 that leaves the range spanned by the
four named poses is reported as "off_path". A finished cycle is
  OK       full sequence, in order, every joint on its path, no ALARM
  ANOMALY  full sequence, but something odd on the way (reasons listed), or a joint reached ALARM
  PARTIAL  back at IDLE without the full sequence      ABORTED  telemetry vanished mid-cycle
A WARN is listed in the cycle's "conditions" but does not by itself make it an ANOMALY.

CONDITIONS  (your numbers, in arm_thresholds.json - the node never invents a limit)
  current: per STATE x JOINT  warn_current / alarm_current  (mA, |current|)  - the same joint draws very different
           current when carrying than when waiting, so one limit per joint would be useless.
  temperature: TEMPERATURE warn / alarm (degC) for all joints, optional per-joint override "J3": {"warn":..,"alarm":..}
  HOLD_S: seconds a value must stay over a limit before it counts.
  MOVING (between poses) and FAULT use the MOVING block (by default the loosest limit of any state).
  It only REPORTS. Hardware STOP limits stay in the Mega / Orchestrator.

RUN
  python3 arm_analyzer_node.py --calibrate --ref riff_raw.csv   open the limits next to the recorded data,
                                                       change them, test them, save them (like --calibrate of the camera)
  python3 arm_analyzer_node.py --write-thresholds      create arm_thresholds.json to edit by hand
  python3 arm_analyzer_node.py                         live (MQTT_HOST env var = broker, default localhost)
  python3 arm_analyzer_node.py --replay riff_raw.csv [--cycles riff_cycles.csv]
                                                       offline: feed a recorded file through the same engine and
                                                       score it against its `phase` column
  python3 arm_analyzer_node.py --show                  last transitions / cycles / conditions from arm_analyzer.db

Install: pip install paho-mqtt        (--replay, --show, --calibrate and --write-thresholds work without it)
Not a safety function: STOP decisions belong to hardware limits and the Orchestrator.
"""
